    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from 960. In the 1120s the Song allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a>, former subjects of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> who had founded the Jin dynasty, and together they conquered the Liao by 1125. Having seen the weakness of the Song army, the Jurchens broke the alliance and began the Jin–Song Wars of 1125 and 1127. The Jin army crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, and in the Jingkang Incident it captured the capital, the retired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-huizong">Emperor Huizong</a>, his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-qinzong">Emperor Qinzong</a> and most of the Imperial court.</p><p>The remaining Song forces regrouped under the self-proclaimed Emperor Gaozong (1127–1162), who withdrew south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and established a new capital at Lin'an (modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>). This shift of capitals is the dividing line between the Northern and Southern Song. The Jin now held the traditional heartlands around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yellow-river">Yellow River</a> and regarded themselves as the rightful rulers of China, choosing earth, the element that follows the Song's fire, as their dynastic element. Pushed south beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a>, the Southern Song still held a large population and productive agricultural land, which sustained a robust economy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a> government sponsored massive shipbuilding and harbour improvement projects, and built beacons and seaport warehouses to support trade abroad through the major international seaports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quanzhou">Quanzhou</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> and Xiamen. Ships sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. Investors spread their money across many ships, and sea trade spurred an enormous growth in the shipbuilding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>. Many West and Central Asian Muslims came to trade and became a preeminent force in the import and export business.</p><p>The Song issued the world's first government <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiaozi">Jiaozi</a>, and government factories printed it in Huizhou, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a> and Anqi; in 1175 the Hangzhou factory employed more than a thousand workers a day. Hangzhou grew from 200,000 people at the start of the 12th century to 500,000 around 1170, and to over a million a century later. Cities had entertainment quarters, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> houses, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> and clubs, and festivals such as the Lantern Festival. Peasants owned their own plots, rented as tenants, or were serfs on large estates. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa-rice">Champa rice</a>, introduced in 1011, gave two harvests a year.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Song dynasty&#8221; cites where it covers Culture and society, Civil service examinations and the gentry, Clothing, Cuisine:</p><ul><li>Jacques Gernet, Daily Life in China on the Eve of the Mongol Invasion, 1250–1276 (Stanford University Press, 1962)</li><li>Patricia Buckley Ebrey, The Cambridge Illustrated History of China (Cambridge University Press, 1999)</li><li>John King Fairbank, China: A New History (The Belknap Press of Harvard University Press, 2006)</li><li>Morris Rossabi, Khubilai Khan: His Life and Times (University of California Press, 1988)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>To protect its shipping the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a> established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s first permanent navy in 1132, with a headquarters at Dinghai. In 1161 the Jin forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanyan-liang">Wanyan Liang</a> (the Prince of Hailing) attacked on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tangdao">Battle of Tangdao</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-caishi">Battle of Caishi</a> the Song navy, with swift paddle wheel-driven vessels carrying traction <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebuchet">trebuchets</a> that launched <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> bombs, won both battles. A century after its founding the navy had 52,000 fighting marines.</p><p>The Song military was organised so that the army could not threaten imperial control, often at the expense of effectiveness. The court often believed that successful generals endangered royal authority, and relieved or even executed them, notably Li Gang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yue-fei">Yue Fei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-shizhong">Han Shizhong</a>. Crossbowmen formed their own prized units, and Song soldiers used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fire-lance">fire lances</a>, iron-cased bombs and other gunpowder weapons. In the war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, the official Li Zengbo wrote in 1259 that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qingzhou">Qingzhou</a> was making one to two thousand iron-cased bombshells a month. The Mongols, in turn, employed northern Chinese soldiers and the same gunpowder weapons against the Song, and from 1268 to 1273 Kublai blockaded the Yangtze River with his navy and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiangyang">Xiangyang</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Song dynasty&#8221; cites where it covers Military and methods of warfare, Gunpowder warfare:</p><ul><li>C. J. Peers, Soldiers of the Dragon: Chinese Armies 1500 BCE–CE 1840 (Osprey Publishing, 2006)</li><li>David Andrew Graff, A Military History of China (Westview Press, 2002)</li><li>Peter Lorge, War, Politics and Society in Early Modern China, 900–1795 (Routledge, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The emperor ruled through a bureaucracy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scholar-officials">scholar-officials</a>. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> period the civil service examinations had become virtually the only means of drafting officials, and the number of candidates for the low-level prefectural exams rose from 30,000 a year in the early 11th century to 400,000 by the late 13th century. Only about 20,000 officials were active, so a larger scholarly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> of candidates, degree-holders, tutors and retired officials took over local affairs, and appointed magistrates relied on their cooperation. Funds for prefectural and county schools came mostly from private financing.</p><p>Finance strained the state. To pay for its defences the government confiscated land from the landed gentry, which cost it loyalty among leading families, while wealthy families with connections in office obtained tax-exempt status. The law kept most of the Tang legal code, and the physician and judge Song Ci (1186–1249) wrote a pioneering work of forensic science on examining corpses. Chancellors held great power late in the dynasty: in 1259 Chancellor Jia Sidao ordered the assault that pushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> back across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>. In 1241, under Emperor Lizong, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a>'s Four Books and his commentary became required study for the examinations.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Song dynasty&#8221; cites where it covers Law, justice, and forensic science:</p><ul><li>Jacques Gernet, Daily Life in China on the Eve of the Mongol Invasion, 1250–1276 (Stanford University Press, 1962)</li><li>Nathan Sivin, Science in Ancient China (VARIORUM, Ashgate Publishing, 1995)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucianism</a> took shape in this age. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhu-xi">Zhu Xi</a> (1130–1200), building on the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheng-yi-philosopher">Cheng Yi</a>, emphasised the Four Books as an introductory corpus to Confucian learning, and his commentary formed the basis of the Neo-Confucian doctrine. Though his writings were not accepted by his contemporaries, by 1241 they were standard study for the examinations, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> adopted his teaching as Shushigaku and Jujahak. The Chan sect published several major koan collections that remain influential in Zen.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> merchant shipping carried iron, swords, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, velvet, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> and textiles across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>; the Moroccan geographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi">al-Idrisi</a> wrote in 1154 of the prowess of Chinese merchant ships there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">Gunpowder</a> weapons developed in the Song wars, and by the 14th century the firearm and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> could be found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a>. Writing flourished too: the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-qingzhao">Li Qingzhao</a> (1084–1151) was famous in her lifetime, and the geographer Fan Chengda (1126–1193) made travel literature popular. Emperor Gaozong commissioned the Eighteen Songs of a Nomad Flute as a diplomatic gesture to the Jin while he negotiated for the release of his mother from captivity.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Song dynasty&#8221; cites where it covers Law, justice, and forensic science, Arts, literature, philosophy, and religion, Polymaths, inventions, and astronomy, Mathematics and cartography:</p><ul><li>Nathan Sivin, Science in Ancient China (VARIORUM, Ashgate Publishing, 1995)</li><li>Jacques Gernet, Daily Life in China on the Eve of the Mongol Invasion, 1250–1276 (Stanford University Press, 1962)</li><li>Patricia Buckley Ebrey, The Cambridge Illustrated History of China (Cambridge University Press, 1999)</li><li>Julius Thomas Fraser, Time, Science, and Society in China and the West (University of Massachusetts Press, 1986)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogedei-khan">Ögedei Khan</a> conquered the Jin dynasty in 1233/34. They had been allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, but the alliance broke when the Song recaptured the former capitals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>. In 1259 Mongol armies attacked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henan">Henan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke Khan</a> died on 11 August during the siege of Diaoyu Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> besieged Ezhou, but a coming civil war with his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a> drew him north, and the Song pushed his forces back across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>. Border skirmishes followed until 1265, when Kublai won a significant battle in Sichuan.</p><p>From 1268 to 1273 Kublai besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiangyang">Xiangyang</a>, the last obstacle to the Yangtze basin, and in 1271 he declared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>. In 1275 a Song force of 130,000 troops under Jia Sidao was defeated by the general Bayan, and by 1276 the Yuan held most of the Song territory, including Lin'an. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yamen">Battle of Yamen</a> on the Pearl River Delta in 1279 the Yuan army under Zhang Hongfan crushed the last resistance; the 7-year-old emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-bing-of-song">Zhao Bing</a> committed suicide with Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-xiufu">Lu Xiufu</a>. Emperor Gong was made Duke of Ying and later exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, where he became a monk, and other members of the family, among them the artist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhao-mengfu">Zhao Mengfu</a>, served under the Yuan.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a> was the Song dynasty after 1127, when the Jin took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china">North China</a> and the court moved to Lin'an. Holding the populous south, it built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s first permanent navy, traded across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> and saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucianism</a> take form, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> conquered it at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yamen">Battle of Yamen</a> in 1279.</em></p>

